ups so there's uh some of the most  important trials in this  have been what they call platform trials  where they're looking at  a large number of different agents so  the together trial for example  um is such a platform trial and they  look at a number of agents there's been  some really good things that have come  out with this but it's not just  you know it's not just everything in the  kitchen sink you have to you know make  choices judiciously with the evidence  that you have and move forward with the  things that have the most promise  in general  when we have now when we're two years in  to this pandemic  there is somewhat of an accepted  standard of care for how moderate to  severe covid is treated in hospital  settings so if we want to introduce the  idea of measuring the effect of  something like ivermectin  would we always be testing it against  the standard of care rather than against  nothing because few people would just  get nothing is that an important aspect  of this  yeah so in um  right so in clinical trials there's  there's a principle of equipoise where  you you don't want to give an inferior  treatment you know ethically you want to  know that you're giving at least the  standard of care  obviously if there is no standard of  care then that's another situation but  if you  have a standard of care you want to be  giving it uh also of course as over the  last year as a lot of people have gotten  vaccinated  and as people have had prior infections  so there's some  protection from immunity and that also  complicates trials there's lots of  things that complicate trials over time  but certainly you want to give  you don't want to  deny the standard of care to somebody in  a clinical trial and again in an ideal  formulation with something like  ivermectin would you really want three  groups one group that is just the  standard of care group and everybody  knows they're not receiving ivermectin  and then you would have a placebo group  and an ivermectin group and they don't  know whether they are receiving the  ivermectin is that was would that be the  ideal setup  so in the  in the first case the chart talking  about where you know people know you  know so it's generally two groups it's  two arms  in in one particular setting even of a  platform trial so in a platform trial  they might be looking at multiple  different settings um  but you want to be able to compare the  people who are getting the standard of  care and the people who are getting the  standard of care and then maybe some new  agent  fair um a few weeks ago you did a great  interview with jaka befemenko  about a study or conference abstract as  maybe it's better referred to on  ivermectin i did a much less detailed a  sec segment on that and this was one  that really caught fire everybody from  jordan peterson to  uh dr john campbell who i i don't say  this pejoratively he's not a medical  doctor i i just i feel driven to say  that because there's some confusion  about about his credentials initially  said wow 70 reduction in mortality for  people given ivermectin you extensively  broke that down including with one of  the authors of the conference abstract  who said this was retrospective  observational data we don't know about  dosing the groups weren't separated by  vaccine status there's all these other  problems with this why do you think that  caught such fire on social media because  the result was just notable and it made  a great headline  yeah so um i mean i'm learning a lot  about how this  misinformation uh  train works and ivermectin seems to be  the grift that keeps on grifting  and  uh  so what happens is in this case it was  just a conference abstract but it had  this  kind of startling result or a result  that could be spun in a way that was  startling and seemed dramatic  and so that little thing gets amplified  into something much larger and then  the results that are presented in the  cons in the conference abstract were  t